## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of amortized analysis with a practical growth strategy, highlighting how linear growth increments behave differently from exponential doubling and how memory copies interact with cache locality.

## Key Concepts
- Amortized cost under linear growth
- Block-based capacity increments (capacity increases by B, not a multiplicative factor)
- Cache locality during bulk copies
- Trade-offs between growth frequency and per-copy work

## Code Example
```javascript
class Queue {
  constructor(capacity = B) {
    this.buffer = new Array(capacity);
    this.head = 0;
    this.tail = 0;
    this.size = 0;
  }

  enqueue(item) {
    if (this.size === this.buffer.length) {
      const newBuffer = new Array(this.buffer.length + B);
      for (let i = 0; i < this.size; i++) {
        newBuffer[i] = this.buffer[(this.head + i) % this.buffer.length];
      }
      this.buffer = newBuffer;
      this.head = 0;
      this.tail = this.size;
    }
    this.buffer[this.tail] = item;
    this.tail = (this.tail + 1) % this.buffer.length;
    this.size++;
  }

  dequeue() {
    if (this.size === 0) return undefined;
    const item = this.buffer[this.head];
    this.head = (this.head + 1) % this.buffer.length;
    this.size--;
    return item;
  }
}
```

## B's Influence
- **Total copies**: Larger B reduces growth frequency, cutting total copy work from O(N²) toward O(N) (since copies ≈ N²/(2B))
- **Cache behavior**: Smaller B keeps working set in cache but triggers more frequent expensive copies; larger B improves sequential access patterns during copies but may evict useful cache contents when reallocating

## Why This Is Asked
Tests streaming algorithm intuition and amortized reasoning. Detecting heavy hitters with bounded memory on a growing stream is a common interview topic; Misra–Gries provides deterministic guarantees with simple updates.

## Key Concepts
- Misra–Gries heavy-hitters
- Choice of k = floor(1/phi) + 1 to bound missed items
- Amortized O(1) per element, O(k) memory
- Trade-offs: larger k reduces false positives but uses more memory

## Code Example
```javascript
class MGHeavyHitters {
  constructor(k) {
    this.k = k;
    this.counters = new Map();
  }
  update(x) {
    if (this.counters.has(x)) {
      this.counters.set(x, this.counters.get(x) + 1);
      return;
    }
    if (this.counters.size < this.k) {
      this.counters.set(x, 1);
      return;
    }
    for (const [key, val] of Array.from(this.counters)) {
      if (val === 1) this.counters.delete(key);
      else this.counters.set(key, val - 1);
    }
  }
  candidates() { return Array.from(this.counters.keys()); }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate the phi-bound with a second pass or a secondary sketch in production?
- How does batch size B influence practical update throughput and memory usage?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to design incremental data structures that handle batch updates with strong worst-case guarantees, and to reason about amortized costs versus rebuilding from scratch.

## Key Concepts
- DSU with parity (union-find with bipartite tracking)
- Incremental graph property maintenance
- Amortized analysis (inverse Ackermann function)
- Batch processing optimization

## Code Example
```javascript
class ParityDSU {
  constructor(n) {
    this.parent = Array.from({ length: n }, (_, i) => i);
    this.parity = new Array(n).fill(0);
    this.rank = new Array(n).fill(0);
    this.isBipartite = true;
  }

  find(x) {
    if (this.parent[x] !== x) {
      const root = this.find(this.parent[x]);
      this.parity[x] ^= this.parity[this.parent[x]];
      this.parent[x] = root;
    }
    return this.parent[x];
  }

  union(x, y) {
    const rootX = this.find(x);
    const rootY = this.find(y);
    if (rootX === rootY) {
      if (this.parity[x] === this.parity[y]) {
        this.isBipartite = false;
      }
      return;
    }
    if (this.rank[rootX] < this.rank[rootY]) {
      this.parent[rootX] = rootY;
      this.parity[rootX] = this.parity[x] ^ this.parity[y] ^ 1;
    } else if (this.rank[rootX] > this.rank[rootY]) {
      this.parent[rootY] = rootX;
      this.parity[rootY] = this.parity[x] ^ this.parity[y] ^ 1;
    } else {
      this.parent[rootY] = rootX;
      this.rank[rootX]++;
      this.parity[rootY] = this.parity[x] ^ this.parity[y] ^ 1;
    }
  }

  processBatch(edges) {
    for (const [u, v] of edges) {
      this.union(u, v);
    }
    return this.isBipartite;
  }
}
```

## Comparison to Recomputing from Scratch
- **DSU with parity**: O(B α(n)) per batch, O(1) query
- **BFS/DFS recomputation**: O(n + m) per batch

The DSU approach provides a significant speedup when B << m, as it reuses information from previous batches rather than recomputing the entire graph structure.

## Why This Is Asked
Maintaining the number of distinct substrings under incremental appends tests knowledge of suffix automata, amortized analysis, and practical update strategies. It highlights how to avoid recomputation and reason about state growth.

## Key Concepts
- Suffix Automaton (SAM) for distinct substrings
- Online extensions and delta = len(cur) - len(link(cur))
- Amortized analysis over batches
- Space linear in string length

## Code Example
```javascript
class SAMNode {
  constructor() {
    this.next = {};
    this.link = -1;
    this.len = 0;
  }
}
class SuffixAutomaton {
  constructor() {
    this.st = [new SAMNode()];
    this.last = 0;
    this.size = 1;
  }
  extend(c){
    const st = this.st; const cur = this.size++;
    st.push(new SAMNode()); st[cur].len = st[this.last].len + 1;
    let p = this.last;
    while (p >= 0 && !(c in st[p].next)) { st[p].next[c] = cur; p = st[p].link; }
    if (p === -1) { st[cur].link = 0; }
    else {
      const q = st[p].next[c];
      if (st[p].len + 1 === st[q].len) {
        st[cur].link = q;
      } else {
        const clone = this.size++;
        st.push(Object.assign(new SAMNode(), st[q]));
        st[clone].len = st[p].len + 1;
        while (p >= 0 && st[p].next[c] === q) { st[p].next[c] = clone; p = st[p].link; }
        st[q].link = st[cur].link = clone;
      }
    }
    this.last = cur;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle a large alphabet or non-text streams efficiently?
- How does the approach adapt if deletions are allowed?
- What are practical bottlenecks in real-world languages?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests dynamic graph indexing and amortized analysis, combining hub labeling with incremental updates and a concrete cost model.

## Key Concepts
- Hub labeling / two-hop cover
- Incremental updates
- Amortized analysis
- Query-time trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode for query
function reachable(S,T,L){
  let best = Infinity;
  for (let h of H[S]) best = Math.min(best, dist(S,h)+dist(h,T));
  for (let h of H[T]) best = Math.min(best, dist(S,h)+dist(h,T));
  return best <= L;
}
```

